  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` didn't handle thousands separators. All 37 `POST /checkout` 500s in the log are the same `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')` at `src/money.js:5`. Every failing payload has a price of `1,005.99`, `1,299.00` or `2,450.50`. The regex didn't match those, so `match()` returned `null` and `m[1]` threw. That's why the errors looked intermittent: only orders with an item priced at 1,000 or more failed.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- Properly grouped amounts like `2,450.50` now parse correctly.\n- Input that still isn't an amount now throws `Error('Invalid amount: …')`, as the function's comment already said it should, instead of a `TypeError`.\n- I didn't change how `/checkout` handles that error, so a malformed price still isn't turned into a 4xx response. The 500s in your log are all the comma case, though.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):** I added three tests:\n- Parsing the three prices from the log.\n- A full checkout containing a comma-formatted price.\n- Rejection of malformed input such as `abc` and `12,34.00`.\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7. I didn't confirm that the new tests fail on the old code.\n\nOne thing I noticed but didn't touch: a logged order, `ord_10180`, shows `total=1015` for 2 × 3.25, which should be 650. I didn't investigate it, so it may be unrelated or may be worth a look.",
